{
  "_doc" : "DisplayGeometry (Shared/DisplayGeometry.swift). Every rect is {x,y,width,height} in ONE top-left-origin pixel space shared by all displays and windows. Section normalize: input {window, display} -> the window's frame normalized 0-1 against that display ((wx-dx)/dw, (wy-dy)/dh, ww/dw, wh/dh); a display with width<=0 or height<=0 gives {0,0,0,0}. Section span: input {displays:[rect]} -> the bounding box of all displays; an empty list gives {0,0,1,1}. Section scenes: input {displays:[{id,isPrimary,rect}], windows:[rect]} -> expect.span (bounding box), expect.spanAspect (span width/height, null if height is 0), expect.displays[i] {id, aspect (width/height, 1 if height is 0), spanFrame (the display normalized into the span: DisplayState.spanFrame)}, expect.windows[i] {displayID (the first display whose rect contains the window centre, x>=minX && x<maxX && y>=minY && y<maxY; else the first isPrimary display; else the first display), frame (the window normalized against that display: WindowState.frame), spanFrame (frame composed onto the display's spanFrame: where the phone's All view draws it)}. A display with no displays to choose from (empty list) gives displayID, frame and spanFrame null; the span is the unit rect and spanAspect 1 (the Mac then normalizes against NSScreen.main, which no vector can pin). A zero-size display (mid-reconfiguration) can still be picked by the primary fallback: the frame is then DisplayGeometry.normalize's zero frame {0,0,0,0}, and the Windows host follows that. Known Mac quirk, not to be copied: the Mac broadcast path (ManagedWindow.toWindowState) sends the window's raw pixel bounds in that case instead; QuipMac/Tests/MacVectorTests.swift pins both. Numbers compare to 1e-9. aspect and spanAspect are DisplayGeometry.aspect(of:) and DisplayGeometry.spanAspect(of:), the functions QuipMacApp.buildDisplayGeometry calls. Expected values are written by the Swift test (QUIP_WRITE_VECTORS=1), never by hand.",
